    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §103, Pub Off L",
    "lawSection": "Public Officers Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S76\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the public officers law, in relation\nto broadcasting open meetings of an agency or department\n\n PURPOSE :  To require meetings of public bodies covered by the Open\nMeetings Law to be streamed in real-time and to be posted on the\nagency's website, to the extent practicable.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 amends Section 103 of the public officers law, by adding a\nnew subdivision (f), which provides that open meetings of an agency\nshall be broadcast to the public and maintained as records to the\nextent practicable, as determined by the agency or the department. If\nan agency maintains a regularly and routinely updated website and\nutilizes a high speed connection, it shall stream its open meetings in\nreal-time and post them on its website within a reasonable time after\nthe meetings, to the extent practicable. This section also provides\nthat an agency may, but is not required to, expend additional funds to\nimplement these provisions.\n\nSection 2 provides that the act shall take effect on the thirtieth day\nafter it shall have become a law.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :  In many instances, open meetings are not reasonably\navailable to be viewed by the public. The time at which they are\nusually scheduled prevents people who work or go to school to\nparticipate. Also, people with physical disabilities might have\nfurther difficulties in participating.\n\nThis legislation would not increase the burdens imposed on agencies.\nPublic bodies would be required to stream and post the meetings only\nif they already maintain a regularly and routinely updated website and\nif they already utilize a high speed internet connection. An agency\nmay, but is not required to, spend additional money to implement the\nprovisions of this legislation. This legislation would help increase\ntransparency by allowing people to virtually participate in open\nmeetings, without imposing additional burdens to state agencies.\nMoreover, making the content of these meetings easily available to the\npublic would reduce the likelihood of repeated questions to the\nagency, the impact of misinformation, and the number of individual\nFOIL requests broadcasting open meetings of an agency or department.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :  2013-14: S.4776/A.6529 Referred to\nInvestigations and Government Operations/Referred to Governmental\nOperations\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS : None\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :  This act shall take effect on the thirtieth day\nafter it shall have became a law.\n",
